note:
Your call (9 Oct): the workarounds are a code smell. Five times this week a publish failed on a test that depended on timing or the real machine, and each was patched by loosening it:
- the offload pace tests' tolerances, twice;
- a tree-cover test's 10 s bound made relative;
- the agent's tests reading the real battery (the build Mac unplugged at 18 %);
- a test assuming the Mac's host name;
- the tree-cover hand-over test ('that lease is gone' under load), now given an explicit waiting mode instead of the clock;
- trees::tests::items_with_parallel_work_of_their_own_never_hang_it: a deadlock check that fails past 60 s of wall time (about 3 s of work), which a 34-minute suite run on a loaded M1 overran (9 Oct). Telling a hang from slowness needs another way than a clock.

Wanted: audit every test (Rust, Python, the web checks) for dependence on wall-clock time, sleeps, the machine's load, its battery, host name, disk, network or real files, and fix the cause:
- decisions made by pure functions, tested on their own;
- tests of an exchange or a loop given an injected clock, policy or environment, not real time;
- no tolerance or bound that only holds on an idle Mac.

Done when the full suite passes on a Mac under heavy load (e.g. run beside a build, or several suites at once) repeatedly, and the earlier loosenings are replaced by proper fixes.
